.TH LOGNAME 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
logname \- print the login name of the current user
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B logname
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B logname
prints the name of the current user, followed by a newline, on standard
output.
It takes no operands.
.PP
The name is the account name that Windows reports for the process, as
the Windows API function
.B GetUserName
returns it.
It has no domain, keeps the case that Windows gives it, and has each
space replaced by an underscore.
.BR id (1)
and
.BR whoami (1)
use the same name for the current user, but in a process that is
elevated and has the Administrators group enabled they print
.B root
instead, as
.BR id (1)
describes.
.B logname
always prints the account name.
.PP
The name does not come from the environment, so setting
.BR LOGNAME ,
.BR USER ,
or
.B USERNAME
does not change it.
.SH OPTIONS
.B logname
takes no options.
Any argument other than
.B \-\-help
as the first argument, including
.BR \-\- ,
a lone
.BR \- ,
and
.BR \-\-version ,
is rejected with a usage message on standard error and exit status 1.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument, and any further arguments
are then ignored.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An argument other than a first
.BR \-\-help ,
or a failure to get the name.
.TP
.B 255
The output could not be written.
No message is printed.
.PP
A rejected argument is reported with a usage message on standard error.
A failure to get the name is reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
logname: getlogin: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
where
.I reason
is
.B "Result too large"
for a name that is too long, as described in
.BR NOTES .
When Windows does not supply the name at all, busybox records no
reason, so the message shows an unrelated reason or ends at
.BR getlogin .
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the name of the current user:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ logname
jdoe
.EE
.RE
.PP
The environment does not change the result:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ USER=nobody LOGNAME=nobody logname
jdoe
.EE
.RE
.PP
For an account named
.BR "Jane Doe" ,
each space becomes an underscore:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ logname
Jane_Doe
.EE
.RE
.PP
Name a directory after the current user:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ mkdir "build-$(logname)"
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from POSIX and GNU
.BR logname :
.IP \(bu 2
There is no login record to read.
The result is the name of the account that Windows reports for the
process, and no terminal is needed.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no
.BR \-\-version ,
and
.B \-\-help
is recognized only as the first argument.
.PP
For an account name with spaces the result differs from the
.B USERNAME
variable, which keeps the spaces.
.PP
The user name is read in the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows 10
version 1903 and later, so on those versions it may contain any Unicode
characters.
On older versions it is limited to the system code page.
A name of 64 bytes or more is not printed and fails with
.BR "Result too large" ,
and one of 100 bytes or more cannot be read at all; see
.BR "EXIT STATUS" .
.PP
The output line ends with LF, not CRLF.
When standard output is a console, a name with non-ASCII characters is
converted for display from the ANSI code page, unless the variables in
.B ENVIRONMENT
say otherwise.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR id (1),
.BR whoami (1)
